    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b2fc4a24_2ce8a19d",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/ResourceTypes.h",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 215,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-06-11T15:48:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Some of these ScratchUsageFlags are more like the default labels we use in the factory, such as RasterBitmapTexture, LazySkImageBitmapTexture, or YUVRasterBitmapPlane. However, depending on who calls these factories, the actual proxy may or may not be scratch.\n\nSimilarly, the flags like \"ChildDevice\" and \"CopyDeviceTexture\" may or may not be scratch but do represent a derived label from the original proxy\u0027s label. I think this points us to a bit of a generalization of `ScratchUsageFlags` to just be `TextureUsageFlags`.  Any texture can have these bits and the effective label of the proxy and/or Texture is the custom label + the usage flags.\n\nWhen we then make a child device copy or a subset texture, we dont\u0027 have to perform string concatenation right then and there, we just set the usage flag + old label.\n\nHowever, we do then need to set the labels at the right time. I think this would be in TextureProxy::instantiate() for non-scratch textures, we\u0027d construct the full label and set it when calling into the ResourceProvider. For scratch textures, it would continue to go through the scratch manager which does the aggregation logic that you have before setting the full label from all the proxies that share the texture.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 215,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 215,
        "endChar": 47
      },
      "revId": "0add26adaf485ad41ed1ef6cc2f9bf11af2252d3",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
